WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.842
A cloud security assessment should ask
whether defenders could detect and

2
00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:11.301
investigate important activity.

3
00:00:11.381 --> 00:00:18.956
Relevant sources may include cloud audit
logs, identity logs, network logs,

4
00:00:19.036 --> 00:00:26.145
data access logs, workload logs, alert
rules, security posture events,

5
00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:29.488
and incident response runbooks.

6
00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:33.764
Logging is not only a compliance checkbox.

7
00:00:33.844 --> 00:00:39.787
It determines what can be reconstructed
during an incident.

8
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:46.510
AI can assist by mapping log sources to
assets, services, and risks.

9
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:52.300
It can summarize which logs exist, which
are centralized,

10
00:00:52.380 --> 00:01:00.537
which have short retention, and which
high-value systems appear to lack coverage.

11
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:08.775
It can also draft questions such as
whether responders could reconstruct identity

12
00:01:08.855 --> 00:01:15.847
activity, network connections, data
access, configuration changes,

13
00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:20.006
and administrative actions for a system.

14
00:01:20.086 --> 00:01:23.000
Retention and access matter.

15
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:30.888
Logs that exist for only a short time may
be gone before an investigation begins.

16
00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:36.678
Logs that are stored but not searchable
may slow response.

17
00:01:36.758 --> 00:01:43.750
Logs that can be modified by the same
identities under review may be less

18
00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.112
defensible.

19
00:01:45.192 --> 00:01:52.068
The assessment should consider whether
logging supports detection,

20
00:01:52.148 --> 00:01:58.907
investigation, evidence preservation, and
operational response.

21
00:01:58.987 --> 00:02:05.047
Detection coverage should be tied to
realistic cloud events.

22
00:02:05.127 --> 00:02:12.119
Suspicious privilege changes, unusual API
activity, new access keys,

23
00:02:12.199 --> 00:02:19.424
exposed storage, unexpected public
endpoints, abnormal data transfer,

24
00:02:19.504 --> 00:02:26.730
disabled logging, and risky configuration
changes are common examples.

25
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.422
AI can help organize detection gaps,

26
00:02:30.502 --> 00:02:37.262
but defenders should validate which alerts
are feasible and useful.

27
00:02:37.342 --> 00:02:41.770
Incident readiness is broader than
logging.

28
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:48.609
It includes runbooks, roles, escalation
paths, evidence handling,

29
00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:54.399
break-glass access, owner contacts, and
recovery plans.

30
00:02:54.479 --> 00:03:01.472
Assessment outputs should be useful to
responders, not just auditors.

31
00:03:01.552 --> 00:03:09.709
A good finding might say that the team
cannot currently reconstruct data access for

32
00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:18.180
a sensitive service, then recommend a
practical logging and retention improvement.
